 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Octree-based Volume Rendering with Wavelet Leaves](6683-octree-based-volume-rendering-with-wavelet-leaves.md)
-A volume rendering technique using an octree data structure for space subdivision, where leaf nodes store volume data in a multi-resolution wavelet format to enable efficient traversal, early termination, and progressive refinement.
+This method renders volumetric data by combining an octree for spatial subdivision with a multi-resolution wavelet representation stored in leaf nodes. It solves the problem of rendering large-scale datasets (gigabytes) on distributed clusters with limited per-node texture memory by enabling early termination of traversal in empty space and progressive refinement of detail. It is distinguished from the baseline Octree-based Volume Rendering by the specific use of 3D non-standard Haar wavelets in leaves to support incremental loading and frustum-clipping-based memory management.
 
 ### PlenOctree Rendering
-A method for real-time rendering of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) by converting a trained NeRF-SH model into a sparse octree representation [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Mechanism:** For each ray, determine ray-voxel intersections in the octree structure, producing a sequence of segments between voxel boundaries with constant density and color, of lengths $\{\delta_i\}$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Early-Stopping:** Stop rendering when the accumulated transmittance $T_i$ is less than $\gamma = 0.01$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Rendering Formula:** Apply NeRF's volume rendering model:
-    $$ C(r) = \sum_{i=0}^{N} T_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \cdot \delta_i)) c_i $$
-    where $T_i = \exp(-\sum_{j=0}^{i-1} \sigma_j \cdot \delta_j)$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Fine-tuned PlenOctree models achieve significantly faster rendering times (e.g., 42.22 ms/frame) compared to existing methods while maintaining comparable image metrics on scenes like Tanks and Temples [S3, unofficial].
+PlenOctree Rendering converts a trained NeRF-SH model into a sparse octree representation for real-time rendering [S3, unofficial]. A PlenOctree stores density and spherical harmonic (SH) coefficients modeling view-dependent appearance at each voxel [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Step:** For each ray, determine ray-voxel intersections in the octree structure, producing a sequence of segments between voxel boundaries with constant density and color, of lengths $\{\delta_i\}$ [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Early-stopping:** At test-time, stop rendering when the accumulated transmittance $T_i$ is less than $\gamma = 0.01$ [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Formula:** Apply NeRF's volume rendering model:
+    $$ C(r) = \sum_{i=0}^{N} T_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i)) c_i $$
+    where $T_i = \exp(-\sum_{j=0}^{i-1} \sigma_j \delta_j)$ [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the tree traversal and the density of the volume. For dense volumes, the overhead of traversing a tree is not compensated by empty space skipping, leading to performance comparable to or worse than non-hierarchical methods [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** High memory footprint for high-resolution regions due to the requirement of keeping entire subtrees resident in cache [S2]. Overhead ratio is 1/3 for 2D downsampling/anisotropic volumes and 1/7 for isotropic cases [S2].
-*   **Performance:** Outperforms other approaches for sparse volumes (e.g., Kingsnake, Beechnut, 3DNeurons) and large numbers of channels (e.g., CyCIF Small) when combined with residency management [S2]. For dense volumes, it may suffer from higher computational cost [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Effective for sparse volumes; simple hierarchical culling; supports LOD via projection size/FOV criteria [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Poor performance on dense volumes; high memory usage for multi-channel rendering; visual artifacts at LOD boundaries; limited by main memory size [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **When to Use:** Sparse volumetric datasets; single-channel rendering; scenarios where empty space skipping is effective [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Dense volumetric datasets; multi-channel rendering with high memory constraints; datasets larger than main memory [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the sparsity of the volume and the efficiency of the traversal. For dense volumes, the computational cost of traversing the tree is not compensated by empty-space skipping, leading to performance comparable to or worse than non-hierarchical methods [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** High memory footprint in multi-channel scenarios due to per-channel tree traversal and subtree residency requirements [S2]. Storing full subtrees for high-resolution regions incurs significant overhead [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** In benchmarks using WebGPU, the octree approach achieved frame rates comparable to residency-based methods for single-channel data but suffered higher computational cost for multi-channel data (e.g., CyCIF Small with 16 channels) [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Effective empty-space skipping for sparse volumes; simple one-to-one mapping [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory overhead for subtrees; visual artifacts at LOD boundaries; inability to handle datasets larger than main memory [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When to use:** Sparse volumes with significant empty space; single-channel rendering where memory bandwidth is not the primary bottleneck [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** Dense volumes; multi-channel rendering requiring high cache efficiency; datasets exceeding main memory capacity [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 ## Background
-The octree spatial hierarchy was first proposed by Chamberlain et al. for rendering surface geometry, later adapted for volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. LaMar et al. developed a similar technique with improved selection criteria based on distance and field-of-view [S1, unofficial]. Weiler et al. proposed extensions to smooth discontinuity artifacts at LOD boundaries [S1, unofficial]. The method is a baseline for comparing newer residency-based and hybrid approaches [S2]. Wavelet-based leaf storage has been explored as a frequency domain analysis method for volumetric data [S1, unofficial].
+The octree spatial hierarchy was first proposed by Chamberlain et al. for rendering surface geometry, later adapted for volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. LaMar et al. developed a similar technique with improved selection criteria based on distance and field-of-view [S1, unofficial]. Weiler et al. proposed extensions to smooth LOD discontinuities [S1, unofficial]. A common problem identified in these early algorithms was the inability to reduce volumetric data size in main memory, limiting scalability [S1, unofficial]. The method is used as a baseline for comparison in modern volume rendering research, such as in the Residency Octree study [S2].
 
